// Part of the `geometry` namespace, see ../namespaces.tc
//
// A namespace is a directory. `import geometry;` loads every .tc file
// directly inside of geometry/ and puts all of their declarations into one
// shared table of names. So the files of a namespace see everything of each
// other without importing anything, and the order of the files does not
// matter (rect.tc uses Point and abs_diff from here).
//
// Since it is one table, a name can only be defined once per namespace.
// A second abs_diff in rect.tc is the same error as defining it twice
// in this file:
//
//   geometry/rect.tc:17:5: error: redefinition of function 'abs_diff'
//   (already defined at geometry/point.tc:29)

// `pub` has nothing to do with the files in here seeing each other. It only
// decides what somebody who imports the namespace can reach as geometry::name
pub typedef struct {
    int x;
    int y;
} Point;

pub Point point(int x, int y) {
    Point p = {x, y};
    return p;
}

// no pub, so this is visible in all files of geometry/ but not outside of it
int abs_diff(int a, int b) {
    return a > b ? a - b : b - a;
}

// The exception to "once per namespace" is `static`. Like in c a static
// function belongs to its file: it is looked up in its own file first and
// is invisible to the other files of the namespace. rect.tc has its own
// static `length` with a different signature and the two do not clash.
// In the generated c code they get the file name in front, so this one
// becomes point_length and the other one rect_length
static int length(Point from, Point to) {
    return abs_diff(from.x, to.x) + abs_diff(from.y, to.y);
}

// an impl block cannot be pub as a whole, each method is marked on its own
impl Point {
    // the distance when you can only walk along the axes
    pub int distance(Self self, Self other) {
        return length(self, other);
    }
}
